            <ArticleTitle>Interscalene block for postoperative analgesia using only ultrasound guidance: the outcome in 200 patients.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>272-7</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jclinane.2008.08.022</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="STUDY OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To report the results of single and continuous interscalene blocks (ISB) performed using ultrasound (US) guidance only.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Prospective, observational study.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">Operating room of a university-affiliated orthopaedic hospital.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">200 ASA physical status I, II, and III patients undergoing shoulder or elbow surgery.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERVENTIONS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Single or continuous ISB were placed using US guidance only. All blocks were performed by anesthesiology residents and supervised by faculty anesthesiologists.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MEASUREMENTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Success rate and frequency of untoward events such as needle paresthesia, vessel puncture, infection, and persistent neurologic deficits were prospectively recorded.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MAIN RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">99% of patients reported sensory and motor changes in the distribution of the brachial plexus and postoperative pain scores &lt;or= 2. The rates of needle paresthesia and vessel puncture were 6% and 1%, respectively. Two patients (1%) reported transient neurologic deficits. No signs or symptoms of infection or intravascular injection were noted. There were also no permanent neurologic deficits.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">In this group of 200 consecutive patients, the success rate for postoperative analgesia using US guidance only was 99%. Untoward events such as needle paresthesia and persistent neurologic deficits were lower than existing data on nerve stimulation and paresthesia techniques. Ultrasound can be successfully used as a &quot;stand alone&quot; method to perform ISB.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
